    <div class="space-y-8">

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          What is a Ratio? Definition, Formulas &amp; Practical Applications
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          A <strong>ratio</strong> is a fundamental mathematical concept describing the quantitative relationship between two or more numbers, indicating how many times one value is contained within another.
          Whether you are comparing the ingredients of a recipe, mixing sand and cement on a construction jobsite, sizing a high-definition video frame, or determining financial valuation multiples, ratios allow us to comprehend relative proportions independent of absolute scales.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- NOTATION CARDS -->
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/60 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950/40 space-y-1.5">
          <span class="text-xs font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 uppercase">1. Colon Notation</span>
          <div class="text-base font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">A : B</div>
          <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
            The standard mathematical and architectural notation (e.g., <strong>3 : 4</strong> or <strong>16 : 9</strong>).
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/60 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950/40 space-y-1.5">
          <span class="text-xs font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 uppercase">2. Fraction Notation</span>
          <div class="text-base font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">A / B or <sup>A</sup>&frasl;<sub>B</sub></div>
          <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
            Represents the ratio as a dividend and divisor, making cross-multiplication direct and intuitive.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/60 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950/40 space-y-1.5">
          <span class="text-xs font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 uppercase">3. Verbal / Word Notation</span>
          <div class="text-base font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">"A to B"</div>
          <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
            Commonly spoken in daily conversation, sports odds, and culinary recipes (e.g., "1 to 2 parts").
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- PART-TO-PART VS PART-TO-WHOLE -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h3 class="text-base sm:text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-2">
          <span>🥗</span> Part-to-Part vs. Part-to-Whole Ratios
        </h3>
        
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950/40 space-y-2">
            <h4 class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 uppercase">Part-to-Part Ratios</h4>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Part-to-part ratios compare two distinct components of a set directly against each other, without regard to the total size of the group.
            </p>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 text-xs">
              <strong class="text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Example:</strong> If a tossed salad contains <strong>5 cucumber pieces</strong> and <strong>4 tomato slices</strong>, the cucumber-to-tomato part-to-part ratio is <strong>5 : 4</strong>. For every 5 pieces of cucumber, there are 4 pieces of tomato, regardless of whether the bowl holds 9 pieces or 900 pieces.
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950/40 space-y-2">
            <h4 class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 uppercase">Part-to-Whole Ratios</h4>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Part-to-whole ratios compare the quantity of one specific component to the cumulative total of all components combined.
            </p>
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 text-xs">
              <strong class="text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Example:</strong> If the total salad bowl contains <strong>50 vegetable pieces</strong> including 5 cucumber slices, the cucumber-to-total ratio is <strong>5 : 50</strong>. Simplified as a fraction, 5/50 equals <strong>1/10 (or 10%)</strong> of the entire salad.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- HOW TO CONVERT RATIO TO FRACTIONS -->
      <div class="space-y-3 rounded-2xl border border-blue-200 bg-blue-50/30 p-5 dark:border-blue-900/40 dark:bg-blue-950/20">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-2">
          <span>🔢</span> How to Convert a Ratio into Fractions &amp; Percentages
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Converting any part-to-part ratio into fractions allows you to determine what fraction of the whole each constituent part represents:
        </p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-xs text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 space-y-1.5 pl-1">
          <li><strong>Sum the ratio terms:</strong> Add all parts together to compute the common denominator: <code>1 : 2 &rarr; 1 + 2 = 3</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Assign numerators:</strong> Place each individual term over the common denominator: <code>1/3</code> and <code>2/3</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Convert to percentages:</strong> Multiply each fraction by 100: <code>(1/3) × 100 = 33.33%</code> and <code>(2/3) × 100 = 66.67%</code>.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

